  {
    "episode_id": 45422,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45422.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, that was produced. It is a bipartisan bill. There aren't very many things that have happened since January and Washington that are truly bipartisan. But protecting children from abuse and neglect is definitely bipartisan. We've put more funding into that. We've also tried to streamline the process to where the government bureaucracies that are on the front lines, whether it be social workers, teachers, law enforcement, people in the court systems, where they can share information easier. One of the things that shocked me was how difficult it was for one bureaucracy in government to communicate with the other bureaucracy. We've also tried to create a list with potential bad actors to where that we can trace people to have a history of being abusive to children to where we know where they are if they move to different locations to try to prevent child abuse. And I think that from an education standpoint, the bill has some good language that will teach people how to better identify children that have potentially been neglected or abused. So I'm real proud of the legislation that passed out of the house last week. And now it's over in the United States Senate. So hopefully they'll take it up very quickly and we can get it to the President's desk very soon.",
